For a real number $k$, consider the system
\begin{align*} |25+20i-z|&=5\\ |z-4-k|&=|z-3i-k| \\ \end{align*}
in the complex unknown $z$, where $i=\sqrt{-1}$. Suppose this system has exactly one complex solution $z$. Adding up all the possible values of $k$ gives $\frac{m}{n},$ with $m$ and $n$ relatively prime positive integers. Find $m+n.$

Let $t$ be a real number such that the system
\begin{align*} |25+20i-w|&=5\\ |w-4-t|&=|w-3i-t| \\ \end{align*}
has exactly one complex solution $w$. The sum of all possible values of $t$ can be written as $\frac{p}{q},$ where $p$ and $q$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $p+q.$ Here $i=\sqrt{-1}.$

Let $k$ be a real number such that the system
\begin{align*} |25+20i-z|&=5\\ |z-4-k|&=|z-3i-k| \\ \end{align*}
has exactly one complex solution $z$. Geometrically, the first equation describes a circle in the complex plane. The sum of all possible values of $k$ can be written as $\frac{m}{n},$ where $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $m+n.$ Here $i=\sqrt{-1}.$

